<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lit de justice</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as bed of justice</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The lit de justice, or bed of justice, was a formal session of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a> under the presidency of the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the Ancien Régime, held to compel the registration of royal edicts and impose the king's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. It took its name from the throne of five cushions under a canopy on which the king sat, surrounded by princes of the blood, peers and prelates. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the setting for trials of great aristocrats; from the 16th century it enforced edicts on a reluctant court, since in the king's presence the parlement lost its role of judge. It was revived under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a>, and the last session was held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> in 1788.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lit-de-justice.jpg" alt="Lit de justice" width="330" height="338" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parlement-Paris-Charles7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
